# Yaya Gemma — Prompt Template & Instruction Standard

Two templates (quick + full), a pre-send checklist, and how to organize your standing instructions so you never repeat them.

---

## A. The quick template (use for ~80% of requests)

Paste and fill. If a line doesn't apply, delete it.

> **Role:** [Lead dev / PM / Social manager / advisor]
> **Scope:** [the ONE thing — one feature, one bug, one doc]
> **What I already know (pinned — don't re-ask these):**
> 1. …
> 2. …
> 3. …
> **Output:** [Lovable .md / reference .docx / inline / one final copy] — for [who/what consumes it next]
> **Before you build:** challenge anything weak or missing.

---

## B. The full template (use for big/expensive tasks: redesigns, audits, multi-file builds)

> **Role:** […]
> **Scope (one unit of work):** […]
>
> **Context / live state:**
> - What's currently deployed or true: […]
> - Which system/file is the live one (if duplicates exist): […]
>
> **The problem / goal:**
> - Symptom or objective: […]
> - The *decision* this output must support: […]   ← lead with this, not the deliverable name
>
> **Pinned decisions (treat as final, do not ask me to choose):**
> - Numbers/limits/prices: […]
> - Free vs premium gating: […]
> - Canonical source doc (if conflict): […]
> - Where I'm unsure: "you choose X, optimizing for [goal]"
>
> **Constraints / guardrails:**
> - [375px / 44px touch targets / no new LLM call if X / allergy filter before scoring / Angela voice / link-yes-or-no]
>
> **Target files (extend, don't rewrite):** […]
>
> **Output format:** [one paste-ready Lovable .md with e2e tests] + [separate reference doc for any TODOs assigned to me]
> **Naming:** businessunit_purpose_code_name_YYMMDD
>
> **Before you build:** diagnose/challenge first, then produce. Flag conflicts with my other AI tools or earlier decisions.

---

## C. Pre-send checklist (10-second scan)

- [ ] **One scope?** If the request has two unrelated jobs, split or say "do part 1 only."
- [ ] **Pinned every lever?** Any number, limit, price, count, gate, deadline, or either/or choice — is it filled in, or explicitly handed to me with a goal?
- [ ] **Everything I already know is in turn one?** No saving tweaks for later messages.
- [ ] **Said who consumes the output?** (Lovable / Claude Design / the team / a Facebook group / an investor.)
- [ ] **Format stated?** File vs inline, one final version vs options.
- [ ] **Right chat?** Is this the same unit of work as the rest of this thread, or does it need a new chat?
- [ ] **(Marketing only)** Link-yes or link-no? How many variants?
- [ ] **(Eng only)** What's deployed? Which file is live? Full audit or focused fix?

---

## D. The handoff block (use this to END a long chat cleanly)

When I suggest starting fresh, ask for this before you leave:

> "Give me a handoff block for the next chat: (1) what we decided, (2) what's still open, (3) which file supersedes which, (4) the exact next step."

Then your **next chat's first message** = that block + the relevant file name. Context preserved, zero re-explaining.

---

## E. How to organize your instructions (so you stop repeating them)

You have three places to put instructions. Right now some things are in the wrong layer, which is why you re-type them.

**Layer 1 — Project instructions / skills (set once, always on).**
Things that are TRUE EVERY TIME belong here, never in a prompt:
- Angela's voice rules, feminine grammar, sole-developer framing
- Tech stack, URL mapping (get / app / hub)
- File naming convention
- The 4-step output format
- Mobile metrics (375px, 44px)
- FEAT-010 guardrails
*(Most of this is already in your project — good. If you ever re-type one of these into a prompt, that's a signal it should move up to this layer.)*

**Layer 2 — The prompt (this request only).**
Things that CHANGE per task:
- The specific scope, the pinned numbers for *this* feature, the target files, who consumes *this* output.

**Layer 3 — Reference files in the project (the memory between chats).**
Decisions and specs that outlive a chat:
- Charter, KB, recipe engine spec, ticket register, this audit.
- Reference them by name in new chats instead of re-explaining.

**The rule of thumb:**
> If you've typed it in **more than 2 chats**, it belongs in **Layer 1** (project instructions), not in your prompt.
> If it's a **decision you'll need again**, it belongs in **Layer 3** (a named file), not buried in chat history.
> Only **per-task specifics** belong in the prompt itself.

---

## F. Worked example — same request, before & after

**Before (your typical opener, ~costs 2–3 rounds):**
> "Improve the substitution feature for free users and make sure it doesn't cost too much in API."

**After (one-shot):**
> **Role:** Lead dev. **Scope:** substitution engine, free tier only.
> **Live state:** FEAT-010 base is deployed; recipeTemplateMerger.ts and fuzzyMatch.ts exist.
> **Pinned:** free tier = exactly 1 substitution; paywall text visible + non-collapsible; NO Gemini call if no local candidate found; allergy/diet filter runs BEFORE scoring.
> **Target files (extend, don't rewrite):** recipeTemplateMerger.ts, fuzzyMatch.ts, UnlockModal.tsx, CookNowOverlay.tsx.
> **Output:** one Lovable .md, e2e tests included.
> **Before you build:** flag any guardrail I've contradicted.

Same information you had in your head either way — but the second one ends in one response instead of three.
